=0ABill=0A=0A01The Shift=0A=0A=0AThe Traceability Handshake Is Becoming a S=
upplier Requirement=0A=0ATraceability evidence now has to survive the bound=
ary between companies.=0A=0AA shipment can reach the dock before its usable=
 traceability record does. The=0Aproduct is physically there. The lot refer=
ences, certificates, event records,=0Aand receiving data that would let the=
 next operator accept it are not, or they=0Aarrive in a form the receiving =
system can=E2=80=99t use. So the team rebuilds the trail=0Aby hand from inb=
oxes, portals, and disconnected systems before it can decide=0Awhether to a=
ccept, hold, investigate, or escalate.=0A=0AMost companies can trace at lea=
st part of a product inside their own=0Aenvironment. The harder problem sta=
rts when that history has to cross into=0Aanother company=E2=80=99s ERP, WM=
S, QMS, MES, or receiving process. Item identifiers=0Adiffer. Lot and seria=
l references don=E2=80=99t match. Certificates travel separately=0Afrom the=
 shipment. Event records use different structures.=0A=0ANIST gave this prob=
lem a current technical foundation on September 9. NIST IR=0A8536=0A[https:=
//www.nist.gov/publications/supply-chain-traceability-principles-manufactur=
ing-meta-framework]=0Alays out a manufacturing traceability meta-framework =
for building a continuous,=0Atime-ordered provenance chain across separate =
supply chain ecosystems. It relies=0Aon common data patterns, interoperable=
 interfaces, cryptographically verifiable=0Alinks, and selective disclosure=
, so companies can share the evidence=0Atraceability requires without expos=
ing proprietary operating data or depending=0Aon a central repository.=0A=
=0AThe framework matters less than what it signals for operators. The shift=
 is from=0Avisibility to usability. A supplier can hold the right informati=
on and still=0Afail the handoff if the receiver can=E2=80=99t connect it to=
 the product, lot, shipment,=0Aevent, or decision inside its own workflow. =
Traceability is becoming part of the=0Asupplier handshake, judged alongside=
 the product, the ASN, and the delivery=0Adate.=0A=0AA test for your receiv=
ing team=0A=0APick one supplier lot from last month. Can your team connect =
it to its PO,=0Ashipment, certificate, receipt, inspection, and downstream =
use without=0Arebuilding the story from email threads and separate systems=
=3F If the answer=0Atakes a day of digging, the handshake is already broken=
.=0A=0AHolding the evidence isn=E2=80=99t the same as handing it over in a=
 form the next=0Acompany can use.=0A=0AFor Cencora, this isn=E2=80=99t a fu=
ture architecture problem. A truck can reach the=0Adock before the data req=
uired to receive its product.=0A=0A02From the Floor=0A=0A=0AThe Truck Is at=
 the Dock. The Data Isn=E2=80=99t.=0A=0AGregg Gorniak of Cencora on receivi=
ng product that arrives before its evidence.=0A=0AGregg Gorniak is Vice Pre=
sident of Manufacturer Operations and Data Services and=0ASecure Supply Cha=
in Lead at Cencora, which receives pharmaceutical shipments=0Afrom manufact=
urer partners. Under the Drug Supply Chain Security Act, Cencora=0Aneeds th=
e matching serialized data before it can receive covered product and=0Atake=
 ownership of it. In a Q&A with Pharmaceutical Commerce=0A[https://www.phar=
maceuticalcommerce.com/view/q-a-a-playbook-for-dscsa-value-beyond-complianc=
e],=0AGorniak described how his team compares purchase orders, ASNs, and ex=
pected=0Adelivery windows against incoming EPCIS data.=0A=0A=E2=80=9CI don=
=E2=80=99t want to be reactive when the truck arrives at my dock and then I=
 can=E2=80=99t=0Abreak the seal and open the truck and get the product off.=
=E2=80=9D=0A=0AGregg Gorniak=0A=0AVP, Manufacturer Operations and Data Serv=
ices, Cencora. Pharmaceutical Commerce,=0ASept. 2026=0A[https://www.pharmac=
euticalcommerce.com/view/q-a-a-playbook-for-dscsa-value-beyond-compliance]=
=0A=0AHis fix is an early warning. A red, amber, and green view flags suppl=
iers whose=0AEPCIS data hasn=E2=80=99t arrived 12 to 24 hours before the tr=
uck is due. That window=0Agives Cencora and the manufacturer time to repair=
 the data handoff, whether the=0Acause was a system glitch or a file nobody=
 pushed, before the shipment reaches=0Areceiving. Without it, the truck is =
physically present and the product is=0Aoperationally unavailable.=0A=0AGor=
niak also draws a line between compliance and a usable handoff. A portal,=
=0APDF, or spreadsheet can satisfy a minimum access requirement and still l=
eave the=0Areceiver stuck, because a PDF can=E2=80=99t be ingested into the=
 system that validates=0Athe product. In his framing, a portal makes a supp=
lier compliant. It doesn=E2=80=99t=0Amake it a good partner.=0A=0AThe takea=
way=0A=0ADSCSA is specific to pharmaceutical distribution, not a rule for e=
very plant or=0Awarehouse. The operating lesson travels anyway: the quality=
 and timing of the=0Atraceability record is now part of the supplier relati=
onship, not an=0Aafterthought to the product and the ASN.=0A=0A03The Stack=
=0A=0A=0AThe Supplier Traceability Reconciliation Agent=0A=0AOne place to r=
ebuild the supplier-to-production evidence chain, with humans=0Areviewing o=
nly the exceptions.=0A=0ASupplier evidence arrives through ASNs, certificat=
es, packing documents, emails,=0Aportals, and shared event records. Interna=
l evidence sits across the PO, the ERP=0Areceipt, the WMS scan, the QMS ins=
pection, inventory movements, and BOM or=0Aproduction consumption. Today th=
e buyer or quality lead spends most of the=0Aeffort matching identifiers an=
d putting events in order before investigating the=0Aactual discrepancy.=0A=
=0AThis agent does the assembly work and hands a person only the missing or=
=0Aconflicting links. It=E2=80=99s a practical system pattern, not a named =
product you can=0Abuy off the shelf.=0A=0AWhat it reads=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ASu=
pplier side: ASN, packing list, certificates, and supporting documents.=0A=
=0A=C2=A0=0A=0APurchasing and ERP: PO line, item, revision, expected quanti=
ty, supplier master,=0Areceipts, and inventory transactions.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=
=0AWarehouse and quality: WMS scans and location moves, plus QMS inspection=
,=0Adisposition, and nonconformance records.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AProduction: M=
ES or BOM consumption records where available.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AOptional: E=
PCIS events, when both supplier and receiver use the standard.=0A=0AHow it =
runs, in five steps=0A=0A1. Select. The operator picks a component, lot, se=
rial number, shipment, or=0Areceipt.=0A=0A2. Normalize. The agent extracts =
and normalizes the supplier and internal=0Aidentifiers.=0A=0A3. Sequence. I=
t orders the available events from supplier shipment through=0Areceipt, ins=
pection, inventory, and production use.=0A=0A4. Compare. It checks supplier=
 claims against internal records and flags=0Amissing, duplicate, late, or c=
onflicting evidence.=0A=0A5. Decide. The buyer or quality lead reviews the =
exceptions and approves the=0Anext action.=0A=0AWhat it returns=0A=0A=C2=A0=
=0A=0AA time-ordered traceability packet with links back to every source re=
cord.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AA broken-link list that shows the first missing or c=
onflicting handoff.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AThe affected material, inventory, WIP,=
 or assemblies the current data supports.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AAn evidence view=
 for the buyer, receiving lead, or quality lead.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AA drafted=
 supplier evidence request, held for human approval.=0A=0AWhat it looks lik=
e on a real receipt=0A=0AA certificate references supplier lot A417. The AS=
N uses a shipment-level=0Aidentifier, and the ERP receipt records an intern=
al lot. The agent links all=0Athree records, checks quantity and revision, =
and finds that the inspection=0Aresult is tied to the wrong internal lot. I=
t sends that exception to quality. It=0Adoesn=E2=80=99t release or hold the=
 material on its own.=0A=0ATwo paths to build it=0A=0APath 1: Internal buil=
d=0A=0ACombine document extraction, an event store, read connectors to ERP,=
 WMS, QMS,=0Aand MES, identifier-mapping rules, optional EPCIS semantics, a=
nd a human=0Aexception queue. Begin with one supplier, one component family=
, and read-only=0Aaccess.=0A=0APath 2: ERP or implementation partner=0A=0AB=
uild the same flow around the supplier-document, lot, receipt, quality, and=
=0Atrace-reporting modules already in place. The operating team keeps owner=
ship of=0Aidentifier mappings, exception rules, and release authority.=0A=
=0AGuardrails=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AIt reconciles the evidence that exists. It c=
an=E2=80=99t make false source data true or=0Aprove that an undocumented ev=
ent happened.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AIt doesn=E2=80=99t release material, change =
a system of record, issue a hold, or contact=0Aa supplier without human app=
roval.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AEvery conclusion keeps its source record and a conf=
idence or gap status.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AEPCIS [https://ref.gs1.org/standards=
/epcis/2.0.0/] gives partners a shared event=0Alanguage. It doesn=E2=80=99t=
 replace ERP, WMS, QMS, MES, or supplier source records.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AC=
ost depends on connector access, document variability, event volume, and=0A=
partner participation, so there=E2=80=99s no universal price. Start read-on=
ly and=0Anarrow.=0A=0AThe first useful version doesn=E2=80=99t need to trac=
e the whole enterprise. It needs to=0Ashow one supplier and one receiving t=
eam exactly where their shared evidence=0Achain breaks.=0A=0ABefore you go=
